{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Hear, O Israel, YHWH our Elohim</b>. This too is a commandment that he comes to explain, for in the utterance 'I am YHWH your Elohim' the unity would be expressed, as they said (Mekhilta, Bo, ch. 5): Rabbi Nathan says — from here is a refutation of the sectarians who say there are two powers, for when the Holy One, blessed be He, stood on Mount Sinai and said, 'I am YHWH your Elohim,' who protested against Him? But he comes to explain this commandment, and he mentioned it after the Ten Commandments because it is the root of faith, and anyone who does not acknowledge it denies the principle, like an idol worshipper. 'YHWH our Elohim, YHWH one' — YHWH, who is our Elohim and not the Elohim of the nations, is destined to be YHWH one, as it is stated (Zechariah 14:9), 'On that day YHWH will be one and His name one' — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 6:4). And you must contemplate this, for the verse changed here to say <b>YHWH our Elohim</b>, and did not say <b>your Elohim</b> as it says everywhere — 'Hear, O Israel: you pass over this day… for YHWH your Elohim' (Deuteronomy 9:1, 3), 'Hear, O Israel: you draw near this day… for YHWH your Elohim' (Deuteronomy 20:3–4), and likewise in all the passages where He speaks with Israel, He mentions 'YHWH your Elohim.' And here too He said, 'And you shall love YHWH your Elohim,' but in the declaration of unity He mentioned <b>YHWH our Elohim</b>, because He performed through Moses the great and awesome deeds to make for Himself a name of glory. And behold, the dalet of אחד is large, to hint at what is written (Isaiah 63:12), 'Who divided the waters before them, to make for Himself an everlasting name.' Therefore they instituted in the recitation of Shema: 'Blessed be the name of His glorious kingdom forever and ever,' and they said a parable of a king's daughter who smelled the fragrance of the cooking-pot, etc. (Pesahim 56a), for Moses said it in the Torah by way of hint, and then turned and said, 'And you shall love YHWH your Elohim,' in the language of the passages of Mishneh Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The acronym ע\"ז is resolved as עבודת זרה (idol worship); the phrase כעובד ע\"ז is rendered 'like an idol worshipper.' The Aramaic loanword מיחה is rendered 'protested.' The large ד of אחד is transliterated as 'dalet.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:46:08.272773824Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}